{"meta":{"course":"dynamics","chapter":"03","section":"04","chapter_title":"Planar Rigid Body Dynamics","title":"Module 4: Rigid Body Impulse and Angular Momentum","description":"Apply linear and angular impulse-momentum principles to rigid bodies, use conservation of angular momentum about a fixed point, and analyze eccentric impact with the coefficient of restitution at the contact point.","learning_objectives":["Apply linear impulse-momentum to the center of mass of a rigid body.","Apply angular impulse-momentum about the center of mass and about a fixed point, choosing the correct moment of inertia.","Identify when linear or angular momentum is conserved during an impact, recognizing impulsive constraint reactions.","Solve eccentric-impact problems using contact-point velocities and the coefficient of restitution along the impact line."],"concept_ids":["impulse_momentum","angular_impulse_momentum","conservation_of_angular_momentum","eccentric_impact","coefficient_of_restitution"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"prereq_section_ids":["dynamics/03/02","dynamics/02/03"],"practice_ids":["rbim_pin_conservation","rbim_skater","rbim_contact_point","rbim_axis_choice","rbim_pinned_rod_numeric"]},"content":"# Rigid Body Impulse and Angular Momentum\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Apply** linear impulse-momentum to the center of mass of a rigid body\n2. **Apply** angular impulse-momentum about the center of mass and about a fixed point, choosing the correct moment of inertia\n3. **Identify** when linear or angular momentum is conserved during an impact, recognizing impulsive constraint reactions\n4. **Solve** eccentric-impact problems using contact-point velocities and the coefficient of restitution along the impact line\n\n---\n\n## Opening: How a Diver Adds a Somersault in Mid-Air\n\nA platform diver leaves the board with a fixed amount of angular momentum, set the instant their feet lose contact. Nothing in the air can change that total. Yet divers routinely speed up to whip through somersaults and then slow down to enter the water clean. They do it by changing their moment of inertia: tucking pulls mass toward the spin axis, which shrinks $I_G$, and since $I_G\\,\\omega$ is conserved, $\\omega$ shoots up. Opening into a layout reverses it.\n\nThat is angular momentum conservation made visible, and it is the heart of this section. When external forces act only briefly or have no moment about a chosen point, the time-integrated form of the laws of motion, impulse and momentum, becomes the cleanest tool available. It is the only convenient framework for collisions, impacts, and the short, violent forces that ordinary $F = ma$ cannot capture.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Impulse-momentum is the third leg of the kinetics triangle. It turns \"an unbounded force acting for an instant\" into a finite, solvable change in momentum.\n\n---\n\n## Why Rigid Body Impulse-Momentum Completes the Kinetics Trio\n\nFor particles, dynamics offers three equivalent kinetics frameworks: Newton's second law (force-acceleration), work-energy (energy bookkeeping), and impulse-momentum (time-integrated force). Each one shines for a different class of problem. The rigid-body extension of impulse-momentum is the missing leg of the triangle for rotating and translating bodies, and is the only convenient tool for problems involving **collisions, impacts, and short-duration forces** in rigid-body dynamics.\n\nHibbeler's chapter 19, Beer/Johnston's chapter 17, and Meriam's chapter 6/7 cover this material. This module assembles the linear and angular impulse-momentum principles for rigid bodies, the principles of conservation, and a worked workflow for impact problems.\n\n---\n\n## Linear Impulse-Momentum: From Particle to Rigid Body\n\nFor a particle, $\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\vec{F}\\,dt = m\\vec{v}_2 - m\\vec{v}_1$. For a rigid body, the same equation holds **for the center of mass**:\n\n$$\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sum \\vec{F}\\,dt = m\\vec{v}_{G,2} - m\\vec{v}_{G,1}$$\n\nwhere $\\vec{v}_G$ is the velocity of the center of mass G. The linear impulse from external forces equals the change in linear momentum of the center of mass. Internal forces between rigid-body particles cancel by Newton's third law.\n\nThis is the same principle as for a particle, applied to the center-of-mass motion. The rigid-body extension is the next equation.\n\n---\n\n## Angular Impulse-Momentum About the Center of Mass\n\nA rigid body's angular momentum about its center of mass G is\n\n$$\\vec{H}_G = I_G \\vec{\\omega}$$\n\nwhere $I_G$ is the centroidal mass moment of inertia and $\\vec{\\omega}$ is the angular velocity. The principle:\n\n$$\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sum \\vec{M}_G\\,dt = I_G \\vec{\\omega}_2 - I_G \\vec{\\omega}_1$$\n\nThe angular impulse from external moments **about the center of mass** equals the change in angular momentum about the center of mass. Internal moments cancel.\n\nFor planar motion this reduces to scalar form: $\\sum \\int M_G\\,dt = I_G(\\omega_2 - \\omega_1)$.\n\n---\n\n## Angular Impulse-Momentum About a Fixed Point\n\nWhen a rigid body rotates about a fixed point O (e.g., a pinned wheel, a pendulum), the principle has a cleaner form using the moment of inertia about O:\n\n$$\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sum \\vec{M}_O\\,dt = I_O \\vec{\\omega}_2 - I_O \\vec{\\omega}_1$$\n\nwhere $I_O = I_G + md^2$ by the parallel-axis theorem and d is the distance from G to O. This works specifically when O is fixed; for moving reference points, use the centroidal form above.\n\n---\n\n## Conservation Laws\n\nWhen the system has no external linear impulse, **linear momentum is conserved**:\n\n$$m\\vec{v}_{G,1} = m\\vec{v}_{G,2}$$\n\nWhen the system has no external angular impulse about a chosen point, **angular momentum is conserved about that point**:\n\n$$\\vec{H}_{O,1} = \\vec{H}_{O,2}$$\n\nThese two principles handle the entire family of impact and short-duration-force problems where impulsive forces dominate and ordinary forces (gravity, friction over a long arm) contribute negligibly during the impact interval.\n\nThe classic example: a spinning ice skater pulls in their arms. No external angular impulse acts; angular momentum about the vertical axis is conserved; pulling arms in reduces $I_G$, so $\\omega$ must increase to keep $I_G \\omega$ constant. The same logic explains the rotational dynamics of pirouettes, satellites changing configuration, and gymnasts on the high bar.\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"rigid-body-impulse-momentum\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## Eccentric Impact\n\nWhen two rigid bodies collide along a line that does not pass through both centers of mass, the impact is **eccentric**. The impact line is the line of action of the impulsive contact force. Two principles structure the analysis:\n\n**Conservation of momentum along the impact line.** No external impulse along the line during impact (gravity acts perpendicular over the brief duration); linear momentum components along the impact line for the system are conserved.\n\n**Coefficient of restitution along the impact line.**\n\n$$e = \\frac{(v'_{B,n} - v'_{A,n})}{(v_{A,n} - v_{B,n})}$$\n\nwhere the primes denote post-impact, the n subscript denotes the impact-line component, and e is the coefficient of restitution (e = 1 perfectly elastic, e = 0 perfectly plastic). For real materials, e is typically 0.4-0.9.\n\n**Velocities perpendicular to the impact line** are unchanged by an idealized impact (no friction during the impulsive interaction, by assumption).\n\nFor each body, the velocity of the **contact point** (not the center of mass) enters the restitution equation, because that is where the impact force acts. For a rotating body, the contact-point velocity equals the center-of-mass velocity plus the rotation contribution $\\vec{\\omega} \\times \\vec{r}_{G\\to{\\rm contact}}$.\n\nThis last point is the most common error in eccentric impact problems: students use the center-of-mass velocity in the restitution equation instead of the contact-point velocity.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Conservation of momentum along the impact line\",\"definition\":\"m*v = m*v1 + M*V, because no external impulse acts along the line during the brief impact\"},{\"term\":\"Angular impulse-momentum about G\",\"definition\":\"J*d = I_G*omega, linking the contact impulse offset from G to the body spin\"},{\"term\":\"Coefficient of restitution at the contact point\",\"definition\":\"e = ((V + omega*d) - v1) / (v - 0), using the contact-point velocity, not the center velocity\"},{\"term\":\"Velocity perpendicular to the impact line\",\"definition\":\"Unchanged by an idealized frictionless impact\"},{\"term\":\"Contact-point velocity of a rotating body\",\"definition\":\"Center velocity plus omega cross r from G to the contact point\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Examples\n\n### Example 1: Pinned Rod Struck by a Ball (Symbolic)\n\nA uniform rod of mass $M$ and length $L$ is pinned at its top end. A ball of mass $m$ moving horizontally with speed $v$ strikes the rod at distance $d$ below the pin. What is the rod's angular velocity immediately after a perfectly plastic impact?\n\n**Step 1: Identify the conserved quantity.** The pin exerts an impulsive reaction during impact. Linear momentum is **not** conserved (the pin reaction has a horizontal component). Angular momentum **about the pin** is conserved (the pin's reaction has zero moment about itself).\n\n**Step 2: Initial angular momentum about the pin** (only the ball contributes; the rod is at rest):\n\n$$H_{O,1} = m v d$$\n\n**Step 3: After the plastic impact** the ball sticks at the strike point. The combined system rotates about the pin with $\\omega'$. Moment of inertia about the pin:\n\n$$I_O = I_{O,\\text{rod}} + m d^2 = \\frac{1}{3}ML^2 + md^2$$\n\n**Step 4: Conservation** $H_{O,1} = H_{O,2} = I_O\\,\\omega'$ gives\n\n$$\\boxed{\\omega' = \\frac{m v d}{\\frac{1}{3}ML^2 + md^2}}$$\n\nThis problem in $F = ma$ form would require integrating impulsive forces over an indefinite short interval. Impulse-momentum gives the answer in four lines.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Plastic Strike on a Pinned Rod: omega = m v d / ( (1/3) M L^2 + m d^2 )\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"M\",\"label\":\"Rod mass\",\"min\":1,\"max\":12,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"kg\",\"default\":6},{\"name\":\"L\",\"label\":\"Rod length\",\"min\":0.5,\"max\":2,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":1.2},{\"name\":\"m\",\"label\":\"Ball mass\",\"min\":0.1,\"max\":2,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"kg\",\"default\":0.5},{\"name\":\"v\",\"label\":\"Ball speed\",\"min\":0,\"max\":40,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"default\":20},{\"name\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Strike distance below pin\",\"min\":0.1,\"max\":2,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":0.9}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Rod inertia about pin I_O\",\"unit\":\"kg*m^2\",\"expr\":\"(1/3)*M*L^2 + m*d^2\"},{\"label\":\"Post-impact omega\",\"unit\":\"rad/s\",\"expr\":\"m*v*d/((1/3)*M*L^2 + m*d^2)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"d\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nSweep the strike distance `d`: omega is not maximized at the very tip, because moving the ball outward raises both the incoming angular momentum (the $m v d$ numerator) and the inertia (the $m d^2$ term in the denominator).\n\n<inline-check question=\"In this pinned-rod impact, why is angular momentum about the pin conserved while linear momentum is NOT?\" explanation=\"During impact the pin exerts a large impulsive reaction with a horizontal component, so the system's linear momentum changes. But that reaction acts AT the pin, giving it zero moment arm about the pin, so it contributes no angular impulse about that point. Angular momentum about the pin is therefore conserved.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The impulsive pin reaction has zero moment about the pin but a nonzero linear component\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The ball is much lighter than the rod\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Gravity is conserved during the impact\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Pinned Rod Struck by a Ball (Numbers)\n\n**Problem:** A uniform rod of mass $M = 6$ kg and length $L = 1.2$ m is pinned at its top. A ball of mass $m = 0.5$ kg moving horizontally at $v = 20$ m/s strikes and sticks to the rod at $d = 0.9$ m below the pin. Find the angular velocity immediately after impact.\n\n**Step 1: Moment of inertia about the pin.**\n\n$$I_O = \\frac{1}{3}ML^2 + md^2 = \\frac{1}{3}(6)(1.2)^2 + (0.5)(0.9)^2 = 2.88 + 0.405 = 3.285 \\text{ kg·m}^2$$\n\n**Step 2: Conservation of angular momentum about the pin.**\n\n$$m v d = I_O\\,\\omega' \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\omega' = \\frac{(0.5)(20)(0.9)}{3.285} = \\frac{9.0}{3.285}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{\\omega' = 2.74 \\text{ rad/s}}$$\n\nThe combined rod-plus-ball system swings up about the pin at 2.74 rad/s. If you had wrongly used $I_G$ of the rod (about its own center) instead of $I_O$ about the pin, you would have badly overstated $\\omega'$, because the rod's mass also has to swing about the offset pin.\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Spinning Skater (Conservation of Angular Momentum)\n\n**Problem:** A skater spins on near-frictionless ice with arms extended, moment of inertia $I_1 = 4.0$ kg·m² and angular velocity $\\omega_1 = 2.0$ rad/s. The skater pulls their arms in, reducing the moment of inertia to $I_2 = 1.5$ kg·m². Find the new angular velocity.\n\n**Step 1: No external angular impulse** about the vertical spin axis (ice is frictionless, gravity has no moment about the vertical axis), so angular momentum is conserved:\n\n$$I_1\\,\\omega_1 = I_2\\,\\omega_2$$\n\n**Step 2: Solve.**\n\n$$\\omega_2 = \\frac{I_1\\,\\omega_1}{I_2} = \\frac{(4.0)(2.0)}{1.5} = \\boxed{5.33 \\text{ rad/s}}$$\n\nThe angular velocity more than doubles, purely from redistributing mass. This is the diver's tuck of the opening, and the same principle that lets a collapsing star spin up into a pulsar.\n\n> **Workflow Reminder:** Choose a point about which the troublesome (impulsive or constraint) forces have zero moment, write angular momentum before and after, and set them equal. Use $I_O$ about a fixed point, $I_G$ about the mass center.\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: Eccentric Impact on a Free Rod (Restitution, Numbers)\n\nThis example exercises the full eccentric-impact workflow: linear momentum, angular momentum about the mass center, and the coefficient of restitution evaluated at the **contact point**. It is the kind of problem that ordinary $F = ma$ simply cannot reach.\n\n**Problem:** A uniform rod of mass $M = 4$ kg and length $L = 1.2$ m rests at rest on a frictionless horizontal surface, free to translate and rotate. A ball of mass $m = 0.6$ kg slides along the surface at $v = 10$ m/s and strikes the rod perpendicular to its length at a point $d = 0.5$ m from the rod's mass center G. The coefficient of restitution is $e = 0.6$. Find the ball's velocity, the rod's mass-center velocity, and the rod's angular velocity immediately after impact.\n\n**Step 1: Set up the unknowns and the FBD logic.** During the brief impact, the only impulsive force is the contact impulse $J$ acting along the impact line (perpendicular to the rod). Friction is absent and gravity is negligible over the impulsive interval. Call the post-impact ball speed $v_1$, rod mass-center speed $V$, and rod angular velocity $\\omega$. All act along or about the impact line.\n\n**Step 2: Linear momentum of the system (along the impact line).** No external impulse acts on the ball-plus-rod system along the line, so\n\n$$m v = m v_1 + M V$$\n\n**Step 3: Impulse on each body.** The rod starts from rest, so the contact impulse equals its momentum change, $J = M V$. The same impulse acts on the rod at offset $d$ from G, so by angular impulse-momentum about G,\n\n$$J d = I_G\\,\\omega, \\qquad I_G = \\tfrac{1}{12} M L^2 = \\tfrac{1}{12}(4)(1.2)^2 = 0.48 \\text{ kg·m}^2$$\n\nCombining $J = MV$ with $Jd = I_G\\omega$ gives $\\omega = \\dfrac{M d}{I_G}\\,V$.\n\n**Step 4: Coefficient of restitution at the contact point.** The rod's contact point moves at $V + \\omega d$ (translation plus rotation), while the ball moves at $v_1$. With the rod initially at rest,\n\n$$e = \\frac{(V + \\omega d) - v_1}{v - 0} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; e v = (V + \\omega d) - v_1$$\n\n**Step 5: Solve the system.** From Step 2, $v_1 = v - \\tfrac{M}{m} V$. Substituting that and $\\omega = \\tfrac{Md}{I_G}V$ into Step 4,\n\n$$e v = V + \\frac{M d^2}{I_G} V - v + \\frac{M}{m} V \\;\\Rightarrow\\; (1 + e)\\,v = V\\left(1 + \\frac{M d^2}{I_G} + \\frac{M}{m}\\right)$$\n\nEvaluate the bracket: $\\dfrac{M d^2}{I_G} = \\dfrac{(4)(0.5)^2}{0.48} = 2.083$ and $\\dfrac{M}{m} = \\dfrac{4}{0.6} = 6.667$, so the bracket is $1 + 2.083 + 6.667 = 9.75$. With $(1+e)v = (1.6)(10) = 16$,\n\n$$\\boxed{V = \\frac{16}{9.75} = 1.64 \\text{ m/s}}$$\n\nBack-substitute:\n\n$$\\omega = \\frac{M d}{I_G}V = \\frac{(4)(0.5)}{0.48}(1.64) = \\boxed{6.84 \\text{ rad/s}}$$\n\n$$v_1 = v - \\frac{M}{m}V = 10 - (6.667)(1.64) = \\boxed{-0.94 \\text{ m/s}}$$\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Work the Eccentric Impact Decision by Decision\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"For the free rod struck off-center on frictionless ice, which momentum statement is valid for the ball-plus-rod system along the impact line?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Linear momentum is conserved: m*v = m*v1 + M*V\",\"Linear momentum is not conserved because of an impulsive reaction\",\"Only angular momentum about the contact point is conserved\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"The rod is free (no pin), so no external impulsive reaction acts along the line. Linear momentum along the impact line is conserved: m*v = m*v1 + M*V.\"},{\"prompt\":\"In the restitution equation, which rod velocity must appear?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The mass-center velocity V alone\",\"The contact-point velocity V + omega*d\",\"The angular velocity omega alone\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Restitution operates on contact-point velocities. The struck point moves at V + omega*d (translation plus rotation), so e*v = (V + omega*d) - v1.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Collecting terms gives (1+e)v = V*(1 + M d^2/I_G + M/m). With the bracket equal to 9.75 and (1+e)v = 16, what is V?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"1.64 m/s\",\"6.84 m/s\",\"16 m/s\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"V = 16/9.75 = 1.64 m/s. Then omega = (M d/I_G) V = 6.84 rad/s and v1 = v - (M/m)V = -0.94 m/s (the ball rebounds).\"},{\"reveal\":\"If you had used V alone (ignoring omega*d) in the restitution step, the rotation would drop out, underpredicting both omega and the kinetic-energy loss. The contact-point velocity is the crux of every eccentric-impact problem.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n**Step 6: Sanity checks.** The negative $v_1$ means the ball rebounds, which is reasonable for a light ball off a heavier rod. Linear momentum balances: $m v_1 + M V = (0.6)(-0.94) + (4)(1.64) = 6.0 = m v$. Kinetic energy drops from $\\tfrac{1}{2}m v^2 = 30$ J to $\\tfrac{1}{2}m v_1^2 + \\tfrac{1}{2}M V^2 + \\tfrac{1}{2}I_G \\omega^2 \\approx 16.9$ J; the 13.1 J loss is consistent with $e = 0.6 < 1$. Had we (wrongly) used the rod's mass-center velocity instead of the contact-point velocity $V + \\omega d$ in Step 4, the restitution equation would have ignored the rotation entirely and badly underpredicted both $\\omega$ and the energy loss.\n\n---\n\n## Common Errors and Their Diagnoses\n\n- **Using F=ma for impulsive forces.** Impulsive forces produce finite impulses over zero (idealized) time intervals. F itself is unbounded; only the impulse $\\int F\\,dt$ is meaningful. Use impulse-momentum, not Newton's second law in instantaneous form.\n- **Forgetting that the pin reaction is impulsive.** When a rigid body has a pinned constraint and is struck, the pin exerts an impulsive reaction. Linear momentum is not conserved; angular momentum about the pin is.\n- **Using center-of-mass velocity in the restitution equation.** Restitution operates on the velocities of the contact points, not the centers of mass. For rotating bodies, contact-point velocity includes the $\\omega \\times r$ term.\n- **Choosing the moment-of-inertia axis incorrectly.** When using angular impulse-momentum about a fixed point O, use $I_O$, not $I_G$. The parallel-axis theorem connects them.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Rigid Body Impulse-Momentum\" context=\"A bullet of mass m and speed v embeds in the end of a uniform rod of mass M and length L pinned at the other end. The student must compute the rod's angular velocity immediately after impact, justifying the choice of conservation principle, the choice of moment-of-inertia axis, and the treatment of the pin reaction.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\nImpulse-momentum is the design-level tool wherever forces are large but brief, or wherever total angular momentum is a conserved budget to be managed.\n\n| Application | What Impulse-Momentum Buys the Engineer |\n| --- | --- |\n| Vehicle crash analysis | Crumple zones lengthen the impact time $\\Delta t$, lowering the peak force for the same momentum change $\\int F\\,dt = \\Delta(mv)$ |\n| Forging and stamping presses | A hammer's momentum sets the deforming impulse delivered to the workpiece per blow |\n| Reaction wheels on satellites | Spinning a flywheel up or down trades angular momentum with the spacecraft body to reorient it without thrusters (zero external moment) |\n| Turbine and propeller blade strikes | Eccentric-impact and restitution models size the impulsive loads and rebound from foreign-object debris |\n| Sports equipment (bats, clubs, rackets) | The \"sweet spot\" is the impact point that minimizes the impulsive reaction felt at the hands, an eccentric-impact result |\n| Pile drivers and demolition | Each drop delivers a momentum-controlled impulse; restitution governs energy lost into the pile versus rebound |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** A reaction wheel and a diver's tuck obey the identical law. With no external moment, total angular momentum is fixed, so changing $I$ changes $\\omega$, and spinning an internal wheel one way turns the body the other. The scale spans from a 60 kg gymnast to a multi-tonne satellite; the conservation statement does not change.\n\n---\n\n## Connecting the Concepts\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"impulse_momentum\",\"angular_impulse_momentum\",\"conservation_of_angular_momentum\",\"eccentric_impact\",\"coefficient_of_restitution\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Linear impulse-momentum | $\\displaystyle\\int \\sum \\vec{F}\\,dt = m\\vec{v}_{G,2} - m\\vec{v}_{G,1}$ |\n| Angular (about G) | $\\displaystyle\\int \\sum M_G\\,dt = I_G(\\omega_2 - \\omega_1)$ |\n| Angular (about fixed O) | $\\displaystyle\\int \\sum M_O\\,dt = I_O(\\omega_2 - \\omega_1)$ |\n| Parallel-axis | $I_O = I_G + Md^2$ |\n| Coefficient of restitution | $e = \\dfrac{v'_{B,n} - v'_{A,n}}{v_{A,n} - v_{B,n}}$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Impulse-momentum is the right tool for impacts and short-duration forces, where $F = ma$ fails.\n- Linear momentum of the center of mass changes by the external linear impulse; internal forces cancel.\n- For a struck pinned body, linear momentum is not conserved (impulsive pin reaction) but angular momentum about the pin is.\n- Use $I_O$ about a fixed point and $I_G$ about the mass center; the parallel-axis theorem connects them.\n- In eccentric impact, the coefficient of restitution uses contact-point velocities, which include the $\\omega \\times r$ term for rotating bodies.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nThis completes the kinetics trio (force-acceleration, work-energy, impulse-momentum) for rigid bodies. Subsequent chapters extend these planar tools toward three-dimensional rigid-body motion and vibrations, where the same conservation reasoning continues to pay off.\n\n---\n\n### Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A uniform rod pinned at its top is struck horizontally at a point below the pin by a ball that sticks to the rod. Which conservation principle applies, and which does not?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Both linear and angular momentum are conserved.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Angular momentum about the pin is conserved (the pin reaction has zero moment about itself). Linear momentum is NOT conserved because the pin exerts an impulsive horizontal reaction during impact.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Neither is conserved.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. The impulsive pin reaction breaks linear-momentum conservation. Angular momentum about the pin is unaffected because the reaction has zero moment about itself, making this the correct conservation principle to invoke.\"></interactive-quiz>\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"rbim_pin_conservation","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"conservation_of_angular_momentum","stem":"A uniform rod pinned at its top is struck horizontally below the pin by a sticky ball. Which conservation principle applies?","options":["Both linear and angular momentum are conserved.","Angular momentum about the pin is conserved (pin reaction has zero moment about itself). Linear momentum is NOT conserved because the pin exerts an impulsive horizontal reaction.","Only kinetic energy is conserved.","Neither is conserved."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The impulsive pin reaction breaks linear-momentum conservation. Angular momentum about the pin is preserved because the reaction's moment about the pin is zero.","misconception_id":"always_conserve_linear_momentum","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["conservation_of_angular_momentum","angular_impulse_momentum"]},{"id":"rbim_skater","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"angular_impulse_momentum","stem":"An ice skater spinning on a near-frictionless surface pulls in their arms. What happens and why?","options":["Angular velocity decreases as work is done against centrifugal force.","Angular velocity increases. With no external angular impulse about the spin axis, angular momentum I_G ω is conserved. Pulling arms in reduces I_G, so ω must increase.","Linear momentum is conserved instead.","Nothing changes."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Conservation of angular momentum: I_G ω is fixed. Reducing I_G forces ω to increase. The same logic explains pirouettes and gymnastic skills.","misconception_id":"moi_does_not_change","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["conservation_of_angular_momentum","angular_impulse_momentum"]},{"id":"rbim_contact_point","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"coefficient_of_restitution","stem":"When applying the coefficient of restitution to an eccentric impact between two rigid bodies, which velocities go into the equation?","options":["Center-of-mass velocities of each body.","Velocities of the contact points (which for a rotating body equal v_G + ω × r from G to contact), along the impact line. Center-of-mass velocity is not the contact-point velocity for rotating bodies.","Average of the two centers' velocities.","Impact line is irrelevant."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Restitution operates at the contact. For rotating bodies, contact-point velocity includes the ω × r term. Using center-of-mass velocity is one of the most common errors in eccentric impact.","misconception_id":"use_com_velocity_in_restitution","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["coefficient_of_restitution","eccentric_impact"]},{"id":"rbim_axis_choice","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"rigid_body_impulse_momentum","stem":"A pinned rod has angular impulse applied. Which moment of inertia should appear in ∫M dt = I(ω₂ - ω₁)?","options":["Always I_G (about the centroid).","I_O (about the fixed pin), connected to I_G by the parallel-axis theorem I_O = I_G + Md². Using I_G for moments about a fixed pin understates the rotational resistance.","Either; they are equal.","Neither; angular momentum has no moment of inertia."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Angular momentum about a fixed pin uses I_O. I_G ω is the centroidal angular momentum; the additional Md²ω contribution comes from translation of G about the pin and is captured by the parallel-axis theorem.","misconception_id":"always_use_centroidal_inertia","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["impulse_momentum","angular_impulse_momentum"]},{"id":"rbim_pinned_rod_numeric","type":"numeric","difficulty":"hard","statement":"A uniform rod of mass M = 6 kg and length L = 1.2 m is pinned at its top end. A ball of mass m = 0.5 kg moving horizontally at v = 20 m/s strikes and sticks to the rod at d = 0.9 m below the pin (perfectly plastic). Compute the angular velocity of the rod immediately after impact.","givens":{"rod_mass":"6 kg","rod_length":"1.2 m","ball_mass":"0.5 kg","ball_speed":"20 m/s","strike_distance":"0.9 m"},"final_answer":{"value":2.74,"unit":"rad/s","tolerance":0.02},"concept_ids":["conservation_of_angular_momentum","angular_impulse_momentum"],"hint":"Conserve angular momentum about the pin: m*v*d = I_O*omega, where I_O = (1/3)*M*L^2 + m*d^2."}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"always_conserve_linear_momentum","pattern":"conservation_overreach","description":"Conserving linear momentum during impacts even when impulsive constraint reactions are present","trigger":"learner conserves linear momentum across a pinned-rod impact","feedback":"Pinned constraints exert impulsive reactions during impact. Linear momentum is NOT conserved. Angular momentum about the pin IS conserved because the reaction has zero moment about itself.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"moi_does_not_change","pattern":"static_inertia_assumption","description":"Assuming a body's moment of inertia about a fixed axis cannot change","trigger":"learner says ω cannot change without external torque","feedback":"Configuration change (pulling arms in, deploying solar panels) changes I_G. With angular momentum conserved, ω must adjust to keep I_G ω constant. This is the skater spin-up effect.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"use_com_velocity_in_restitution","pattern":"wrong_velocity_in_eqn","description":"Using center-of-mass velocity in the coefficient-of-restitution equation for an eccentric impact","trigger":"learner writes e = (v_G,B' - v_G,A') / (v_G,A - v_G,B) for rotating bodies","feedback":"Restitution operates at the contact point. For rotating bodies the contact-point velocity equals v_G + ω × r_G→contact. Using v_G alone misses the rotational contribution at the contact.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"always_use_centroidal_inertia","pattern":"axis_confusion","description":"Using centroidal moment of inertia I_G when the angular momentum is taken about a fixed point","trigger":"learner writes ∫M_O dt = I_G (ω₂ - ω₁) for a pinned body","feedback":"Angular momentum about a fixed pin uses I_O = I_G + Md² by the parallel-axis theorem. Using only I_G underestimates the rotational resistance.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"ccf99f4dd7f7abb6af991d7eafd5e75ad6a8c2308e0c99963d34ea4cee0639e8","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}